Manifest O: The Open-Handed Knowing

I will not mistake certainty for truth,
or uncertainty for ignorance.

A memory is evidence, not a recording.
A feeling is real, but it does not prove its source.
An image can clarify—and it can alter what I later recall.

I will ask what supports a belief,
what might have shaped it,
and what could change my mind.

I will keep the difference between
I saw it, I remember it, I imagine it,
and I inferred it—
even when those threads cross.

I do not need perfect certainty to act.
I need honest confidence, proportionate to the evidence,
and the willingness to update.

Hold the possibility open. Hold the evidence close.